#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:319
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Not all devices support all capabilities (typically cordless devices do not "
"provide resolution switching, and of course only cordless devices support "
"cordless status reporting), so some parts of the tab will not be enabled for "
"some mouse types."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:326
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If the mouse supports resolution switching, the <guilabel>Sensor Resolution</"
"guilabel> radio button group will be enabled, and you can switch from "
"<guilabel>400 counts per inch</guilabel> to <guilabel>800 counts per inch</"
"guilabel> and back. If you use <guilabel>800 counts per inch</guilabel>, the "
"same physical movement of the mouse will cause a greater (roughly double) "
"amount of motion of the cursor. This tends to be popular amongst gamers."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:335
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If the mouse supports cordless reporting, the <guilabel>Battery Level</"
"guilabel> and <guilabel>RF Channel</guilabel> widgets will be enabled. You "
"can only change the <guilabel>RF Channel</guilabel> if your mouse has two "
"channel support."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:342
#, no-c-format
msgid "Fixing permission problems on Logitech mice"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:344
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Because of the way USB devices work, the code that accesses the current "
"status on Logitech mice needs to be able to write to the mouse. This should "
"be handled by your distribution, but if not, you may need to do some "
"configuration yourself."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:351
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On a &Linux; system, you should use the hotplug system to change the "
"ownership and permissions on the mouse entry in <filename>/proc/bus/usb</"
"filename>. One way to do this is to create a short script (<filename>/etc/"
"hotplug/usb/consoleUserPerms</filename>) that changes the ownership and "
"permissions, as shown below:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:360
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<![CDATA[\n"
"#!/bin/bash\n"
"#\n"
"# /etc/hotplug/usb/consoleUserPerms\n"
"#\n"
"# Sets up newly plugged in USB device so that the user who owns\n"
"# the console according to pam_console can access it from user space\n"
"#\n"
"# Note that for this script to work, you'll need all of the following:\n"
"# a) a line in the file /etc/hotplug/usb.usermap or another usermap file\n"
"#    in /etc/hotplug/usb/ that corresponds to the device you are using.\n"
"# b) a setup using pam_console creates the respective lock files\n"
"#    containing the name of the respective user. You can check for that\n"
"#    by executing \"echo `cat /var/{run,lock}/console.lock`\" and\n"
"#    verifying the appropriate user is mentioned somewhere there.\n"
"# c) a Linux kernel supporting hotplug and usbdevfs\n"
"# d) the hotplug package (http://linux-hotplug.sourceforge.net/)\n"
"#\n"
"# In the usermap file, the first field \"usb module\" should be named\n"
"# \"consoleUserPerms\" to invoke this script.\n"
"#\n"
"\n"
"if [ \"${ACTION}\" = \"add\" ] && [ -f \"${DEVICE}\" ]\n"
"then\n"
"# New code, using lock files instead of copying /dev/console permissions\n"
"# This also works with non-kdm logins (e.g. on a virtual terminal)\n"
"# Idea and code from Nalin Dahyabhai <nalin@redhat.com>\n"
"    if [ -f /var/run/console.lock ]\n"
"    then\n"
"        CONSOLEOWNER=`cat /var/run/console.lock`\n"
"    elif [ -f /var/lock/console.lock ]\n"
"    then\n"
"        CONSOLEOWNER=`cat /var/lock/console.lock`\n"
"    else\n"
"        CONSOLEOWNER=\n"
"    fi\n"
"    if [ -n \"$CONSOLEOWNER\" ]\n"
"    then\n"
"        chmod 0000 \"${DEVICE}\"\n"
"        chown \"$CONSOLEOWNER\" \"${DEVICE}\"\n"
"        chmod 0600 \"${DEVICE}\"\n"
"    fi\n"
"fi\n"
"]]>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:363
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The usermap file that goes with this is <filename>/etc/hotplug/usb/"
"logitechmouse.usermap</filename>, as shown below:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:370
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<![CDATA[\n"
"# script           match_flags idVendor idProduct bcdDevice_lo bcdDevice_hi "
"bDeviceClass bDeviceSubClass bDeviceProtocol bInterfaceClass "
"bInterfaceSubClass bInterfaceProtocol driver_info\n"
"# Wheel Mouse Optical\n"
"consoleUserPerms     0x0003      0x046d   0xc00e    0x0000       0xffff      "
"0x00         0x00            0x00            0x00            "
"0x00               0x00               0x00000000\n"
"# MouseMan Traveler\n"
"consoleUserPerms     0x0003      0x046d   0xc00f    0x0000       0xffff      "
